title: 'Aegis APPROVE GUI S2/S3, with two mediums: requester text promoted to a card title; buried pile keys are clickable'
summary: 'The markdown renderer for model text is sound: allowlisted h() only, no links or images, sanitised leaves, bounded nesting, and a 10k match cap. Click routing uses the button''s own data-card-id, and z-order puts earlier cards on top. M1: S3 promotes card line 1 (requester-controlled for peer/session cards) to a bold title while moving requestedBy into a hover tooltip, so a requester can present kernel-looking prose. M2: when the pile isn''t fanned, a buried card''s keys are clickable with its head hidden, and the re-layout race can slide the next card''s key under the pointer. L1: an uncited voice.turn.user can name a session. L2: the agent can bold lookalike status labels. Fixes go to the builder before S4. The F2–F6 builder has F4/F5 in (5c7e3877) and is working on F6 with a clientApiId move.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#gui'
learnings:
  - 'Visual hierarchy is a security property: promoting untrusted text to a heading and demoting provenance to a tooltip is a spoofing vector even when the bytes are sanitised.'
  - Overlapping interactive UI (piles) needs pointer-events gating and a re-layout click guard.
